\subsection{Forward-Pass Interventions}
\label{sec:forward_pass_method}

The SGS results establish that gradient-level interventions cannot eliminate CIVO (\S\ref{sec:f6}). We test two forward-pass interventions that operate without modifying gradient flow.

\paragraph{Temporal Conditioning Noise (TCN).}
During imagination, Gaussian noise $\mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ is added to the temporal embedding output before it enters the RSSM, where $\sigma$ is specified as a fraction of the embedding's running standard deviation. By degrading the precision of $\dt$-conditioning at the representation level, TCN prevents the critic from exploiting precise but inaccurate temporal predictions without modifying any gradient pathway. We test $\sigma = 0.5$ and $\sigma = 0.2$.

\paragraph{Reality-Anchored Value Estimation (RAVE).}
An exponential moving average (EMA) tracks the critic's value estimates on real environment states. During imagination, any value estimate exceeding the EMA anchor by more than a ceiling factor is clipped: $V_{\text{clipped}} = \min(V_{\text{imag}}, \text{EMA} \times c)$. This directly limits critic overestimation without affecting the world model's representation. We test $c = 2.0$ and $c = 1.5$.

